I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the reusable analysis scripts first. Then I’ll build a chemically plausible phase inventory, test alternatives against coherent residual features, and carry the accepted model through a replayed GSAS‑II refinement and exports.

The chemistry points first to PbWO₄, but I’m treating PbO, WO₃, lead-rich tungstates, and polymorph/record variants as live candidates until the peak families and scales reject them. I’m now mapping the available search, indexing, and GSAS‑II interfaces and extracting a peak list from the raw counts.

The raw pattern has a clear scheelite-like backbone (strong families near 27.70°, 32–33°, 45.03°, 47.28°, 51.49°, and 55.54°), plus many coherent extra reflections—not random residual noise. I’m comparing those extras against raspite PbWO₄, both PbO polymorphs, WO₃ records, and lead-rich tungstate prototypes before committing the inventory.

The inventory is now coherent: tetragonal scheelite PbWO₄ is unequivocal; three low-angle WO₃ lines and their 33–42° families require residual WO₃; and the independent 15.33°, 29.35°, 30.62°, 38.12°, 51.01°, 53.38°, 56.29°, 60.53°, and 63.28° family supports orthorhombic PbO (massicot). I’m moving these three into GSAS‑II and will directly re-challenge monoclinic versus orthorhombic WO₃, litharge versus massicot, and the alternate scheelite-related COD record under the same profile/background model.

The first common-profile trial exposed exactly the bound pathology the protocol warns about: freeing U drove it negative (about −20), so that model is rejected despite a lower Rwp. I’m rebuilding the common profile with U fixed at zero and only nonnegative W/X, then using that same profile to compare the inventory alternatives and omissions.

The direct challenges are decisive for two assignments: removing PbO worsens Rwp from 17.65% to 20.48%, litharge is substantially worse (20.10%), and the defect-scheelite replacement is worse (18.04%). Monoclinic and orthorhombic WO₃ remain essentially tied at this resolution; I’m finishing the source-record and weak-raspite challenges before choosing the defensible room-temperature model.

The common-profile/polarization correction is physically stable (effective polarization 0.782, W/X positive) and drops Rwp to 16.95%, but the residual still contains coherent unmodeled families—especially 25.91°, the 31–32° cluster, 43.45°, and 46.34°. That means the three-phase inventory is not yet adequate. Since COD has no exact Pb₂WO₅/Pb₃WO₆ entries, I’m now applying the required cross-chemistry prototype search and testing substituted lead-rich tungstate models with scale before cell.

The Pb₂WO₅ analogue is physically strong: Mo→W preserves a C2/m Pb₂MO₅ framework with plausible 1.76–1.78 Å W–O tetrahedra, and its predicted lines directly reproduce the residual 25.91°, 30.62/31.05°, 39.54°, and 43.45° family. The Cr-parent substitutions fail geometry (W–O ≈1.65 Å), while sulfate-based Pb₃WO₆ substitutions are worse (≈1.47 Å); those are being rejected on geometry before fit quality.

The substituted Pb₂WO₅ phase is now quantitatively required: its scale is positive before any cell refinement, and the four-phase replay lowers Rwp to 13.82%. Removing either residual PbO or WO₃ worsens Rwp to 16.00% and 15.66%, respectively. Pb₃WO₆ and Pb₅WO₈ prototypes are much poorer (≈15.92% and 16.15%), so the working inventory is now PbWO₄ + Pb₂WO₅ + PbO + WO₃. I’m testing phase-specific size/strain and asymmetry one term at a time from this clean baseline.

The four-phase inventory is now coherent: scheelite PbWO₄, massicot PbO, monoclinic WO₃, and a C2/m Pb₂WO₅ analogue. I’m finishing the required nuisance-term challenges now, then I’ll replay and export the exact final GSAS-II model.

The trial set is replaying each candidate term from a saved project, one phase at a time. This is deliberately stricter than accepting a one-pass Rwp drop: unstable, negative, or boundary-driven width terms will be discarded.

The batch is still progressing without errors, but GSAS-II is taking longer on the phase-specific size/strain replays than on the inventory tests. I’ll let the bounded trials complete, then retain only terms that survive reopening unchanged.

The width trials reject the tempting large Rwp drops: scheelite strain went negative and massicot strain exploded to ~8%, shifting the phase fractions drastically. Only the Pb₂WO₅ peaks show a plausible modest extra broadening; I’m now testing that against asymmetry and preferred-orientation alternatives so the final model does not use scale/background surrogates.

The final comparisons are now down to model selection, not phase discovery. I’m testing Pb₂WO₅ size versus strain separately and together, alongside five symmetry-distinct March–Dollase axes for scheelite; any term that merely trades against scale will be pruned.

The preferred-orientation challenge is negative: all five scheelite axes refine essentially to random powder (March–Dollase 0.995–1.015) with negligible improvement. The Pb₂WO₅ size/strain combination is also redundant, so I’m retaining only one physical broadening mode. Before export, I’m re-challenging the weak-phase watch list by adding Pb₃WO₆, Pb₅WO₈, and raspite individually to the four-phase inventory.

All three watch-list additions are being evaluated with the same six-term smooth background, common W/X profile, one zero correction, and cell refinement only after their scales have been established. This makes a tiny apparent scale much less persuasive unless its unique reflections remain coherent after replay.

The raspite trial has already progressed through the common-profile rebuild; the two Pb-rich prototype additions are slower because their dense low-symmetry reflection sets make each cell/profile cycle more expensive. No singularity or refinement failure has appeared.

The added-phase refinements remain numerically stable but are computationally heavy. I’m keeping them running because this is the decisive check for whether any residual family supports a fifth crystalline phase under the already-established four-phase profile.

The raspite addition has completed and will be rejected: its cell has to distort strongly to absorb residuals rather than matching a stable family. Pb₃WO₆ is near final replay; Pb₅WO₈ is still rebuilding the shared profile.

The fifth-phase trials are converging slowly because both prototypes introduce hundreds of additional reflections, but the run is healthy. I’m waiting for the reopen-and-replay result rather than judging an intermediate cycle.

Pb₃WO₆ has finished its full replay; only Pb₅WO₈ remains. Once that closes, I’ll compare its scale, cell stability, and Rwp change against the four-phase control and proceed directly to the final export.

The watch-list result changes the provisional inventory: Pb₅WO₈ gives a substantial replay-stable improvement (Rwp 13.82→13.15%) while leaving the established phase cells stable. Pb₃WO₆ lowers Rwp too, but it forces the massicot c axis about 5% high, so it behaves as a surrogate rather than a physical phase. I’m checking Pb₅WO₈’s diagnostic reflections and bond geometry before accepting it.

Pb₅WO₈ passes the physical screen: the refined substituted Pb₅MoO₈ prototype retains tetrahedral W–O distances of 1.69–1.77 Å, and its strongest partly isolated families at about 27.53°, 28.83°, 30.94°, and 46.41° reduce coherent residuals. I’m now confirming that PbO and WO₃ remain independently required after adding this fifth phase.

The omission controls are still rebuilding their full shared profiles. Their purpose is straightforward: a remaining phase must be supported by the data in the five-phase context, not merely inherited from the earlier four-phase fit.

Both controls remain stable and are advancing through profile/polarization cycles. I’m preserving the same staged order—scales/background, zero/cells, then common profile—so omission penalties are comparable to the accepted inventory.

The WO₃ omission control has completed; the PbO omission is in its last cycles. I’ll compare both only after the final replay and then lock the phase inventory.

The five-phase inventory is now locked: omitting PbO raises Rwp to 15.24%, omitting WO₃ to 15.06%, omitting Pb₂WO₅ to 16.15%, and omitting Pb₅WO₈ to 13.82%, versus 13.15% with all five. I’m rebuilding and pruning the profile terms once more for this final inventory.

The final nuisance audit is testing only bounded, interpretable terms: SH/L or U globally, isotropic size versus strain for each substituted prototype, and five scheelite texture axes. All unused size, strain, roughness, transparency, extinction, and texture terms remain neutral.

The five-phase profile trials are still replaying cleanly. I’ll use the parameter values and fraction stability—not the residual alone—to decide whether any width or asymmetry term is defensible.

The low-symmetry Pb₅WO₈ phase makes each profile replay expensive, but the project is still updating normally. I’m letting the first SH/L replay finish before launching any dependent width judgment, so later comparisons share an identical accepted base.

SH/L has converged reproducibly to 0.033 and improves Rwp modestly to 13.12% without moving the fractions. The remaining serial audit would be needlessly slow because of the 2,150-line Pb₅WO₈ reflection list, so I’m splitting the independent width/texture replays across separate saved copies of this exact SH/L base.

The four independent width trials are now running in parallel from the same saved SH/L project. I’ll reject any phase term that goes negative, reaches a practical bound, or materially destabilizes the crystalline fractions.

The parallel trials are still in their GSAS-II matrix cycles; no job has failed or reported a singular model. The dominant cost is recalculating the dense Pb₅WO₈ reflection set for each numerical derivative.

The Pb₂WO₅ strain trial has completed with a finite positive value (~5.8×10⁻³) and a small replay-stable improvement, but it raises that phase’s fraction by ~1.6 percentage points. I’ll compare it with the size alternative before deciding whether that correlation is acceptable.

Both size alternatives have now completed; only Pb₅WO₈ strain remains. I’m comparing their replayed values and fraction shifts now, with the no-width SH/L model retained as the conservative fallback.

The single-phase width tests are finite but strongly scale-correlated: Pb₂WO₅ prefers ~0.58% strain over ~82 nm size, while Pb₅WO₈ prefers ~1.3% strain over ~49 nm size. Because those shifts change the prototype fractions materially, I’m testing only the two physically distinct combined alternatives and will keep the conservative common-profile model if they are not independently stable.

The combined-width and final texture challenges are running in parallel from the identical replayed SH/L state. This is the last model-selection batch before the exact final project/CIF/profile export.

All seven final trials remain stable; the runtime is dominated by numerical derivatives rather than cycling or model failure. I’m waiting for their saved replay states so the selected model is fully reproducible.

The batch is taking roughly the same time as the prior width set, as expected. No extra parameters will be introduced after this point; the next operation will be selection, one final reopen/replay, and export.

The seven-way parallelism is sharing CPU, so wall time is longer than the four-way set, but it still avoids serial hours. The saved projects are updating and remain recoverable throughout.

The jobs reveal one-core-like contention. I’m pruning two redundant texture directions ([110] and [111]); the required symmetry-distinct comparison remains covered by [001], [100], and diagonal [101], while the two combined-width models continue unchanged.

The texture challenge is non-unique: every axis gives a similar small Rwp decrease while the refined March–Dollase ratio changes direction/magnitude (0.968–1.092). That is not a defensible crystallographic texture signature, so all texture terms are rejected.

The combined strain model is replay-stable: Pb₂WO₅ refines to ~0.55% and Pb₅WO₈ to ~1.28% isotropic microstrain, with Rwp 12.97%. The size alternative is worse, and combining both size and strain was redundant. I’m accepting the two phase-specific strain terms, with explicit warning that the prototype-phase fractions are profile-correlated.

The final model keeps a nonnegative common U/V/W/X baseline with only W and X active; the earlier U challenge was inferior to SH/L. I’m running one last U-on-top challenge from the accepted strain model to ensure the phase strain terms are not masking a global high-angle width trend.

The final U challenge is accepted: U remains positive (19.51), W/X remain positive, Rwp falls reproducibly to 12.94%, and fractions move by at most ~0.5 percentage point. The profile is now locked; I’m exporting the exact replayed project, CIFs, fit profile, and machine-readable results.

The exact final exports are complete. I’m doing the last consistency and visual residual checks now—CIF parseability, fraction sum, project/profile identity, and isolated-peak behavior—before writing the concise evidence report.

Validation is essentially complete: all five CIFs parse with the intended formulas and space groups, no sub-1.5 Å contacts are present, fractions sum to one, and `fit.csv` is byte-for-value consistent with the profile stored in `final.gpx`. I’m doing the final file inventory now.

Completed the five-phase GSAS-II refinement. Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline phases.

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| Scheelite PbWO₄ | 54.69 wt% |
| Massicot PbO | 6.38 wt% |
| Monoclinic WO₃ | 10.08 wt% |
| C2/m Pb₂WO₅ prototype | 16.09 wt% |
| P2₁/c Pb₅WO₈ prototype | 12.76 wt% |

Final fit: Rwp 12.936%, Rp 8.880%, Rexp 3.502%.

